    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a clear mandate: leverage speed against vulnerable delivery windows to bridge our category gap in stolen bases. **Trea Turner** (77.33) is our undisputed anchor; he benefits from a premium matchup against Dollander, whose slow delivery profile boosts **Trea Turner**'s already elite theft probability to 94%. Similarly, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (71.93) has been elevated to a \"Strong\" start despite his lower batting order spot. Given that Rocker is notoriously susceptible to the running game, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**\u2019s 100% stolen base expectation is the most potent weapon in our lineup today. We are also capitalizing on **Kyle Schwarber** (70.60), whose xwOBA of .394 against Dollander suggests he is well-positioned to drive in the base runners set up by **Trea Turner**.\n\nThe most contentious roster decision involved the UTIL slot, where **Junior Caminero** (55.41) just narrowly edged out the rest of the bench. While **Junior Caminero** faces a tough arm in Early, the park factors and favorable wind conditions provided the necessary leverage to surpass the production ceilings of our other alternatives. Our \"marginal\" options, including **Dillon Dingler** (56.04) and **Gary Sanchez** (50.19), are relegated to this tier primarily due to the SP_xERA of their opponents, though their dynamic platoon advantages keep them safely above the benching threshold. We continue to monitor the status of **Christian Yelich**, **Hyeseong Kim**, **Colt Keith**, and **Zac Veen**, all of whom remain unavailable, leaving our depth dangerously thin. Be advised: **Yordan Alvarez** (54.97) remains in the lineup despite a sub-optimal L/L matchup against Lodolo; his elite .425 xwOBA acts as our \"break-glass-in-case-of-emergency\" power floor, though he carries the highest volatility risk in our current order.",
